CAP = {}
CAP[1] = r"""\mainfigure{Figure.1.pdf}{fig:reprogramming-overview}{Reprogramming states and relative PAS position. S is the source-like state, R the source-exit state, E the epithelial or mixed-transition state, P the pluripotency-acquisition state and M the mesenchymal state used as a within-library reference, and the prefixes h and m mark human-only and mouse-only families. DWUI is a gene's usage-weighted position on a 0--1 axis spanning its primary-catalog PASs, a relative PAS position rather than $3'$ UTR length. X--Y denotes X minus Y, and negative $\Delta$DWUI is proximal. \textbf{a}, Joint UMAP of 551,201 cells from seven studies, colored by 14 display families (ECM, extracellular matrix; IFN, interferon), with within-study routes (arrows) and observed route starts (open circles). \textbf{b}, Optimal-transport associations between display families, not demonstrated lineage transitions (solid, main association between adjacent observations of a study; dotted, minor association below the display threshold; dashed, association across an interval with unresolved intermediates; dash-dot, association conditioned on a selected P endpoint; dark, human; brown, mouse). \textbf{c}, Mean gene-centered DWUI of the metacell of each cell on within-species expression maps (each gene's DWUI minus its mean within the study, averaged over measured genes; blue, more proximal, and orange, more distal than the study average). Colors compare states within a study, not routes, and the human map pools studies with and without P. \textbf{d}, Upper, mean $\Delta$DWUI of R, E, P and M relative to each route's observed S ($\pm0.03$ spans half a cell, clipped; asterisk, Liu2020, whose libraries pool reprogramming stages; ASC, adipose stromal cells). Lower, documented-source P--S means per series on available genes (circles) and on 1,306 one-to-one orthologs measurable in every series (ticks), with the range over 16 combinations of measurement setting and gene background (gray), and the distal excess on available genes, the percentage of genes with $\Delta\mathrm{DWUI}\geq+0.02$ minus that with $\Delta\mathrm{DWUI}\leq-0.02$ (gray, range over the 16 combinations).}"""
CAP[2] = r"""\mainfigure{Figure.2.pdf}{fig:apa-recurrence-context}{Within-species recurrence, strict $3'$ UTR length changes and response composition. P is the pluripotency-acquisition state, S the source-like state and M the within-library mesenchymal reference. X--Y denotes X minus Y, and negative $\Delta$DWUI is proximal. A gene recurs in a direction when at least two studies support it at the stated $|\Delta\mathrm{DWUI}|$ threshold, every evaluable series agreeing in sign. \textbf{a}, Proximal and distal recurrent genes per species and reference against measurement-matched nulls: observed/null-mean ratio (log scale; circle 0.02, diamond 0.05) with the 95\% null range at 0.02, the count against the null mean at 0.02 (n/exp.) and the distal share against its null range. \textbf{b}, Upper, observed/null-mean ratios of P--S recurrence at 0.02 (human filled, mouse open) under four controls, each against its own null (bootstrap intervals excluding zero, thinning to 20 PAS UMIs, cell-cycle-score matching and a common PAS axis per species, with permutation 95\% ranges), and under the full nested-background null (null mean $\pm$1.96 SD; Supplementary Figure~S6a). Lower, recurrent P--S genes kept in the same direction when bootstrap intervals had to exclude zero, genes with well-level DWUI $q<0.05$ among all tested genes and among tested recurrent mouse P--M members in Schiebinger2019--2i, and the median number of recurrent genes in pseudo-contrasts between same-state halves of S or of P, which share no biological change, against the median observed P--S recurrence on the same background (Supplementary Figure~S6c,d,g,h). \textbf{c}, Recurrent P--S genes whose primary PASs lie in one annotated terminal $3'$ UTR: shortening and lengthening against a stratified null (observed/expected, 95\% range) and the usage-weighted change in retained $3'$ UTR length per series (mean filled, median open). \textbf{d}, Documented-source P--S genes measurable in both studies of a species, of which those responding in both at $|\Delta\mathrm{DWUI}|\geq0.02$ on a common PAS axis are classed by same or opposite direction of APA and of gene expression (GEX, $|\Delta\log_2(\mathrm{CPM}+1)|\geq0.25$). Tiles give counts and observed/null-mean ratios under the response-identity null, and the tested class is outlined. Below, observed/null-mean ratios of the tested class for response identity (whether the same genes respond) and direction (whether responders agree in sign), with 95\% null ranges and BH $q$ over eight tests at right. \textbf{e}, Documented-source P--S effects on 1,306 one-to-one orthologs: mean $\Delta$DWUI split into proximal and distal contributions with the net mean (diamond), and per direction the percentage of genes with $|\Delta\mathrm{DWUI}|\geq0.02$ and their mean (filled), median (open), quartiles (thick) and 10th to 90th percentiles (thin). Study means weight each species' studies equally, and the human--mouse difference in net mean (0.034) is the sum of the differences in the proximal (62\%) and distal (38\%) contributions.}"""
CAP[3] = r"""\mainfigure{Figure.3.pdf}{fig:cross-species-apa}{Direction pairing of recurrent orthologs between species. P is the pluripotency-acquisition state, S the source-like state, E the epithelial or mixed-transition state and M the within-library mesenchymal reference. X--Y denotes X minus Y, and negative $\Delta$DWUI is proximal. Pairings use one-to-one orthologs measurable in at least two studies per species and a full-assay matched null. \textbf{a}, Observed/expected counts at $|\Delta\mathrm{DWUI}|\geq0.02$ for the four tested pairings of human (left square) and mouse (right square) directions (blue proximal, orange distal), with 95\% null ranges (filled, $q_8<0.05$, BH over the eight tested pairings). \textbf{b}, The same at nine thresholds from 0.010 to 0.050 (filled, $q_{72}<0.05$, BH over all 72 pairing--threshold tests). Human-proximal, mouse-distal pairs appear in \textbf{a} only. \textbf{c}, Shared P--M proximal pairs among orthologs tandem-$3'$ UTR in both species (315) or alternative-last-exon-compatible in both (52): observed/expected at 0.02 (circle, filled $q_4<0.05$) and 0.05 (diamond, filled $q_{36}<0.05$) with the 95\% full-assay null range at 0.02, and across nine thresholds (filled, $q_{36}<0.05$). In \textbf{b} and \textbf{c}, points with fewer than one expected pair are omitted. The measurement and PAS-capacity null is compared in Supplementary Figure~S10c. \textbf{d}, \textit{PDLIM7} in human (Xing2020) and \textit{Pdlim7} in mouse (Guo2019--CIP and Schiebinger2019--2i): Ensembl 116 short- and long-coding transcript models (blue, short-isoform last exon containing the proximal PAS; orange, long-isoform last exon; thick boxes, coding sequence; dotted lines, PASs matched within 25 nt), the usage share at or beyond each position, counting the two PASs only, in S, P and M (step curves, whose plateau is the distal share), and native $\Delta$DWUI per study (below $-0.3$ drawn at the scale end). \textbf{e}, Spearman $\rho$ between human and mouse rank effects on homologous PASs with common P/M/S contributors (upper, mean of the two Xing2020 pairs, 216 genes) or P/E/M contributors (lower, Liu2020 with Guo2019--CIP, 116 genes), with ortholog-block bootstrap 95\% intervals. Squares mark $q_8<0.05$ under a coarse and a capacity-conditioned null. Below, the mean mouse rank effect within each of eight equal-count bins of the human rank effect, per study pair (bars, s.e.).}"""
CAP[4] = r"""\mainfigure{Figure.4.pdf}{fig:apa-regulatory-sites}{Local interval change, gene-wide net shift and predicted-site retention. P is the pluripotency-acquisition state, S the source-like state and M the within-library mesenchymal reference, and X--Y denotes X minus Y. Inclusion $I$ is the fraction of terminal-family PAS counts retaining a complete interval. Strict changes have same-sign $|\Delta I|\geq10$ percentage points (pp) at 20 and 50 unique molecular identifiers, and P--S is relative to observed S. \textbf{a}, Direction agreement and Spearman $\rho$ of matched interval pairs, overall and for microRNA (miRNA) and RNA-binding protein (RBP) sites, with the agreeing and all pairs and the genes contributing them. \textbf{b}, Mouse P--M genes with a strict recurrent interval: gene-wide $\Delta$DWUI signed along the interval change and taken from the study with the smaller value (square-root axis), with class counts. \textbf{c}, Genes with $|\Delta\mathrm{DWUI}|<0.02$ and maximum difference in proportion change (MPRO) $\geq0.10$ in both studies (filled, strict recurrent interval; shading, $|\Delta\mathrm{DWUI}|<0.01$ in both). \textit{Trip12} is detailed in Supplementary Figure~S15. \textbf{d}, \textit{Riok3}: terminal-family PAS usage in M and P (step curves, the share of family usage at or beyond each position), paired P--M inclusion of the shared 889-nt interval (shading; thin lines, library pairs; thick lines, library-equal means; $n$, library pairs, native above and inclusion below) and the predicted sites in it of pluripotent-stem-cell miRNAs and of P-expressed RBPs with mouse-derived (direct) or ortholog-transferred human (transferred) motifs, counted as site positions. \textbf{e}, \textit{Fgfr1op2}: P--M $\Delta$DWUI split into terminal-family position, family mass and outside-family terms (diamond, total) and $\Delta I$ of its 970-nt interval (3 and 21 paired libraries). \textbf{f}, Genes with net loss or gain of weighted predicted sites (bars) and each gene's net change in weighted sites (dots).}"""
